("CNJ-011",C,"'Since' + a starting point; 'for' + a length of time. Use the present perfect.","SINCE 2020 (a point). FOR 5 years (a length).","'Since' also means 'because': 'Since it's late, let's go.'","Write a sentence with 'since' + a year.",None,"since vs for","Since + start point (since Monday). For + length (for three days)."),
("CNJ-012",C,"'Unless' already means 'if not', so don't add another negative.","UNLESS = IF NOT.","'Unless' is common in warnings: 'Unless you study, you'll fail.'","Write a warning using 'unless'.",None,"unless vs if not","Unless you hurry = if you don't hurry. Same meaning; 'unless' is shorter."),
("CNJ-013",C,"For a past action in progress, use the past continuous with 'while'.","WHILE = during the time that.","'While' can also show contrast: 'While I like tea, I prefer coffee.'","Write a sentence using 'while I was…'.",None,"while vs during","While + clause (while I was cooking). During + noun (during dinner)."),
("CNJ-014",C,"Don't add 'but' after 'whereas'.","WHEREAS = while on the other hand.","'Whereas' is formal and common in comparisons and legal writing.","Write a sentence comparing two people using 'whereas'.",None,"whereas vs while","Same meaning for contrast. Whereas is more formal."),
("CNJ-015",C,"After 'if', use the present tense for a future meaning.","IF + present, WILL + verb: 'If it rains, we will stay.'","First conditional: real future possibility.","Write a sentence starting 'If it rains tomorrow,…'.",None,"if vs whether","If = condition. Whether = choice between two ('I don't know whether to go')."),
("CNJ-016",C,"'Until' already means 'up to the time when', so 'when' is extra.","UNTIL = up to that time.","'Until further notice' = until you are told otherwise.","Write a sentence using 'until' with a future event.",None,"until vs by","Until = continuing up to (wait until 5). By = deadline (finish by 5)."),
("CNJ-017",C,"After 'when', use the present tense for the future, not 'will'.","WHEN + present, WILL + verb.","'When' introduces time; 'if' introduces a condition that may not happen.","Write a sentence starting 'When I finish…'.",None,"when vs if","When = it will happen. If = it may happen."),
("CNJ-018",C,"After 'whenever', use the present tense even for future meaning.","WHENEVER = every time that.","'Whenever you're ready' = no rush.","Write a sentence starting 'Whenever I feel tired,…'.",None,"whenever vs when","When = one time. Whenever = every time, or any time."),
("CNJ-019",C,"After 'before', use the present tense for the future.","BEFORE + present: 'Before you leave, call me.'","'Before' is also a preposition: 'before lunch'.","Write a sentence starting 'Before you leave,…'.",None,"before vs ago","Before = earlier than something. Ago = back from now (two days ago)."),
("CNJ-020",C,"After 'after', use the present tense for the future.","AFTER + present: 'After I finish, I'll call.'","'Afterwards' = later, at the end of a sentence.","Write a sentence starting 'After I finish work,…'.",None,"after vs afterwards","After + clause or noun (after lunch). Afterwards alone (we ate afterwards)."),
("CNJ-021",C,"Don't use 'as' (reason) and 'so' together.","AS = because (formal).","'As' also means 'like' or 'when': 'as a child', 'as I walked in'.","Write a sentence starting 'As it was late,…'.",None,"as vs like","As + clause or role (as a teacher). Like + noun for similarity (like a teacher).","As I was busy, so I didn't call.","As I was busy, I didn't call."),
("CNJ-022",C,"For an unreal comparison in formal English, use 'were': 'as if he were the boss'.","AS IF = pretending or imagining.","In speech, 'as if he was' is also common.","Write a sentence using 'as if' about someone's behaviour.",None,"as if vs like","As if + clause (as if he were the boss). 'Like' + clause is informal."),
("CNJ-023",C,"For an unreal comparison, use 'were': 'as though she were happy'.","AS THOUGH = AS IF.","Used in stories to describe appearances.","Write a sentence using 'as though'.",None,"as though vs as if","Same meaning. As though is slightly more formal."),
("CNJ-024",C,"'So that' needs a full clause with a subject and verb.","SO THAT + subject + can/could.","'In order to' + verb is a shorter option: 'in order to pass'.","Write a sentence using 'so that I can…'.",None,"so that vs so","So that = purpose (study so that I can pass). So = result (I studied, so I passed)."),
("CNJ-025",C,"'In order that' needs a full clause, not 'to'.","IN ORDER THAT + clause. IN ORDER TO + verb.","Very formal; 'so that' is more common.","Write a formal sentence using 'in order that'.",None,"in order that vs in order to","In order that + clause. In order to + base verb."),
("CNJ-026",L,"Use a full stop or semicolon before 'however', and a comma after it.","HOWEVER: . However, … (full stop, capital, comma).","'However' can also mean 'in whatever way': 'However you do it, be careful.'","Write two sentences linked with 'However,'.",None,"however vs but","But joins two clauses in one sentence. However starts a new sentence (formal)."),
("CNJ-027",L,"Don't use 'because' and 'therefore' together.","THEREFORE = for that reason.","Common in maths and logic: 'Therefore, x = 5.'","Write two sentences linked with 'Therefore,'.",None,"therefore vs so","Same meaning. Therefore is formal; so is everyday."),
("CNJ-028",L,"'Moreover' stands alone, followed by a comma.","MOREOVER = MORE + OVER: adding more.","Formal writing: essays, reports.","Write two sentences linked with 'Moreover,'.",None,"moreover vs also","Same idea. Moreover is formal and adds a stronger point.","Moreover than this, the house is old.","Moreover, the house is old."),
("CNJ-029",L,"Use a full stop or semicolon before 'furthermore', and a comma after it.","FURTHER + MORE: adding a further point.","Formal writing only; 'also' in speech.","Write two sentences linked with 'Furthermore,'.",None,"furthermore vs moreover","Same meaning and formality."),
("CNJ-030",L,"'Nevertheless' shows the opposite of what you'd expect: he did NOT give up.","NEVER-THE-LESS: not any less because of it.","Formal; 'still' or 'even so' in speech.","Write two sentences linked with 'Nevertheless,'.",None,"nevertheless vs however","Similar. Nevertheless stresses 'in spite of that'."),
("CNJ-031",L,"Use a semicolon or full stop before 'nonetheless', and a comma after.","NONE-THE-LESS: none the less for it.","Same as 'nevertheless'.","Write two sentences linked with 'Nonetheless,'.",None,"nonetheless vs nevertheless","Same meaning. Both formal."),
("CNJ-032",L,"'Otherwise' already carries the condition, so 'if' is extra.","OTHERWISE = if not.","'Otherwise' also means 'in other ways': 'The food was cold, but otherwise good.'","Write a warning using 'otherwise'.",None,"otherwise vs unless","Otherwise follows the action (Hurry, otherwise…). Unless comes before the condition (Unless you hurry…)."),
("CNJ-033",L,"Use 'instead of' + -ing, not 'instead to'.","INSTEAD OF + -ing. INSTEAD alone at the end.","'Let's walk instead' = walk rather than the first plan.","Write a sentence ending with 'instead'.",None,"instead vs instead of","Instead alone (we walked instead). Instead of + noun/-ing (instead of driving)."),
("CNJ-034",L,"'Likewise' is an adverb meaning 'in the same way'. To compare with a person, use 'like'.","LIKEWISE = in a LIKE WAY.","'Likewise!' alone = the same to you: 'Nice to meet you.' — 'Likewise.'","Write a sentence using 'Likewise' as a reply.",None,"likewise vs like","Like + noun (like my brother). Likewise alone (She did likewise).","Likewise my brother, I like cricket.","Like my brother, I like cricket."),
("CNJ-035",L,"'Similarly' and 'like' together repeat the idea.","SIMILAR-LY: in a SIMILAR way.","'Similarly' links two sentences with the same idea.","Write two sentences linked with 'Similarly,'.",None,"similarly vs likewise","Same meaning. Similarly is more common in writing.","Similarly like his father, he is tall.","Like his father, he is tall."),
("CNJ-036",L,"Don't use 'because' and 'consequently' together.","CONSEQUENCE-LY: as a CONSEQUENCE.","Formal: reports and essays.","Write two sentences linked with 'Consequently,'.",None,"consequently vs so","Same meaning. Consequently is formal."),
("CNJ-037",L,"Don't combine 'thus' and 'so'.","THUS = so, in this way.","'Thus' is formal and old-fashioned in speech.","Write a formal sentence using 'Thus,'.",None,"thus vs this","Thus = so / in this way. This = a pronoun."),
("CNJ-038",L,"Don't combine 'hence' and 'therefore'.","HENCE = for this reason (formal).","Indian maths: 'Hence proved.' 'Three years hence' = in three years (old use).","Write a formal sentence using 'Hence,'.",None,"hence vs therefore","Same meaning. Hence is more formal and can go before a noun: 'hence the delay'."),
("CNJ-039",L,"Don't combine 'accordingly' and 'therefore'.","ACCORDING-LY: ACCORDING to what was said.","'Act accordingly' = act in the right way for the situation.","Write a sentence using 'act accordingly'.",None,"accordingly vs according to","Accordingly = as a result / suitably. According to = as said by."),
("CNJ-040",L,"Before a noun, use 'in addition TO'; 'in addition' alone starts a new sentence.","IN ADDITION TO + noun. IN ADDITION, + sentence.","Common in formal letters and reports.","Write a sentence starting 'In addition to…'.",None,"in addition vs in addition to","In addition, … (alone). In addition to + noun."),
("CNJ-041",L,"Don't drop 'the': 'on the other hand'.","ON THE OTHER HAND: think of your two hands — two sides.","Often used after 'on the one hand'.","Write two sentences using 'On the one hand … On the other hand…'.",None,"on the other hand vs on the contrary","On the other hand = another side (both true). On the contrary = the opposite is true."),
("CNJ-042",L,"'In contrast' must introduce a difference.","CONTRAST = difference.","Used in comparisons and essays.","Write two sentences comparing cities with 'In contrast,'.",None,"in contrast vs on the contrary","In contrast = comparing two different things. On the contrary = correcting a wrong idea."),
("CNJ-043",L,"Always add a comma after 'for example' at the start of a sentence.","FOR EXAMPLE = e.g.","'For example' can go at the start, middle or end.","Write a sentence using 'for example' to give one example.",None,"for example vs such as","For example + full sentence. Such as + list of nouns (fruits such as mangoes)."),
("CNJ-044",L,"Add a comma after 'for instance' at the start of a sentence.","FOR INSTANCE = for example.","Slightly more formal than 'for example'.","Write a sentence starting 'For instance,…'.",None,"for instance vs for example","Same meaning. Use either."),
("CNJ-045",L,"'Agree' is a verb by itself, so no 'am'.","IN FACT = actually.","'In fact' adds a stronger or surprising point.","Write a sentence starting 'In fact,…'.",None,"in fact vs actually","Same meaning. Actually is more common in speech."),
("CNJ-046",L,"'Words' must be plural: 'in other words'.","IN OTHER WORDS = to say it differently.","Useful for explaining something again more simply.","Write a sentence using 'In other words,'.",None,"in other words vs that is","Same meaning. 'That is' (i.e.) is more formal."),
("CNJ-047",L,"'As a result of' is followed by a noun or -ing; 'as a result' alone starts a new sentence.","AS A RESULT (, sentence). AS A RESULT OF (+ noun).","Common in reports and news.","Write a sentence using 'as a result of'.",None,"as a result vs as a result of","As a result, … (alone). As a result of + noun."),
("CNJ-048",L,"Use 'on the contrary' only to disagree or correct, not to agree.","CONTRARY = the opposite.","'On the contrary' corrects a wrong belief: 'Not boring? On the contrary, it's exciting!'","Write a two-line dialogue using 'On the contrary'.",None,"on the contrary vs on the other hand","On the contrary = the opposite is true. On the other hand = another side, both true."),
("CNJ-049",C,"With 'either…or', the verb agrees with the nearer subject.","EITHER … OR: pick one of two.","'Either' alone: 'You can take either.'","Write a sentence using 'either … or'.",None,"either vs neither","Either = one of two (positive). Neither = not one and not the other (negative)."),
("CNJ-050",C,"'Neither' goes right before the first item: 'likes neither tea nor coffee'.","NEITHER … NOR: not this, not that.","'Neither do I' = I don't either.","Write a sentence using 'neither … nor'.",None,"neither…nor vs either…or","Neither…nor = not both (negative). Either…or = one of two (choice)."),
("CNJ-051",C,"'Both…and' takes a plural verb.","BOTH … AND: two together, plural.","'Both' shows you mean the two together.","Write a sentence using 'both … and'.",None,"both…and vs either…or","Both…and = two together. Either…or = only one."),
("CNJ-052",C,"When 'not only' starts the sentence, invert: 'Not only is he…'.","NOT ONLY … BUT ALSO: this AND that too.","Common in formal speech and essays.","Write a sentence using 'not only … but also'.",None,"not only…but also vs both…and","Similar. Not only…but also stresses the second item more."),
("CNJ-053",C,"Use 'whether…or', not a mix of 'if' and 'whether'.","WHETHER … OR: two choices.","'Whether or not' = in either case.","Write a sentence using 'whether … or'.",None,"whether vs weather","Whether = choice. Weather = rain, sun, etc. Same sound."),
("CNJ-054",C,"Use the present tense after 'provided that', even for the future.","PROVIDED THAT = only if.","Formal; 'as long as' is more common in speech.","Write a condition using 'provided that'.",None,"provided that vs if","Similar. Provided that = only if (a strict condition)."),
("CNJ-055",C,"Use the present tense after 'as long as' for the future.","AS LONG AS = only if, or during the time that.","'As long as you're happy' = only that matters.","Write a sentence using 'as long as'.",None,"as long as vs if","Similar. As long as = only if (a condition that must stay true)."),
("CNJ-056",C,"'Even if' already introduces the contrast, so drop 'but'.","EVEN IF = whether or not.","Used for possible situations, not facts.","Write a sentence starting 'Even if it rains,…'.",None,"even if vs even though","Even if = possible (even if it rains). Even though = true fact (even though it's raining)."),
("CNJ-057",C,"After 'such that', use a full clause with a subject and verb.","SUCH THAT = in a way that.","Formal and often used in maths.","Write a formal sentence using 'such that'.",None,"such that vs so that","Such that = with the result that (formal). So that = purpose."),
("CNJ-058",C,"'In case' already carries the condition, so 'if' is extra.","IN CASE = to be ready if.","'Just in case' = as a precaution.","Write a sentence using 'just in case'.",None,"in case vs if","In case = prepare before (take an umbrella in case it rains). If = only then (if it rains, take one)."),
("CNJ-059",C,"'Given that' already sets up the reason; drop 'but'. Keep both clauses in the same tense.","GIVEN THAT = considering that.","Formal and common in arguments.","Write a sentence starting 'Given that…'.",None,"given that vs because","Similar. Given that = considering this fact (formal)."),
("CNJ-060",C,"'Granted (that)' admits a point before disagreeing, so the next part should contrast.","GRANTED = I accept that, BUT…","'Granted, …' is common in debates.","Write a sentence starting 'Granted, … but …'.",None,"granted that vs although","Similar. Granted that = I admit this, but… (used in arguments).","Granted that he was late, so I was angry.","Granted, he was late, but he had a good reason."),
